  "question": {
    "text": "Roots of the equation $x^2+b x-c=0(b, c\\gt0)$ are",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "pq4c8",
      "text": "both positive",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "MPxzI",
      "text": "both negative",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "QkjrI",
      "text": "of opposite sign",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Fv2cv",
      "text": "None of the above",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "We know that, if roots are of same sign $\\Rightarrow$ Product of root is + ve if roots are of opposite sign $\\Rightarrow$ Product of roots is -ve Here, $x^2+b x-c=0$ (b, $c\\gt0$ )\nProduct of roots $=\\frac{-c}{1}=-\\mathrm{ve}$\n$\\therefore$ Roots are of opposite sign.",
    "image": null
  },
